    = note: `picked` comes from `io::read_to_string`: whoever sends it chooses the value, so nothing the compiler can see says what it holds.
    = note: What is known here does not exclude `names.len()` can be 3, `picked` can be -1, for which `0 <= picked < names.len()` fails.
    = help: Check it where it arrives, with a guard the program handles: `throw BadInput(picked) if !(0 <= picked && picked < names.len())`, or the early `return` that rejects it. After that line, the index is proved.
error[NK1202]: `n >= 0` is a claim about data from outside the program.
   --> tests/outside_data_through_a_result_and_a_field.nika:35:2
    |
 35 | 	assert(n >= 0)
    |  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    |
    = note: `n` comes from outside the program, so nothing the compiler can see says what it holds.
    = note: That it is wrong is a case the program has to handle, not a defect an `assert` catches.
    = help: Check it where it arrives, with a guard the program handles: `throw BadInput(n) if !(n >= 0)`, or `return … if !(n >= 0)`. After that line, this `assert` is proved.
